# Words 51-100. Tuple: id, forms, why, trick, usage, own, (before, during, after), cw, diff, [overrides]
R = [
("VRB-051","cook – cooked – cooked – cooking","'Yesterday' puts the action in the past, so the verb needs -ed.","A COOK COOKS in the kitchen: the person and the action share one word.","'What's cooking?' can also mean 'What's happening?' in casual talk.","Write a sentence about a dish you can cook.",("cut the vegetables","heat them in a pan","serve the food"),"cook vs cooker","A cook is a person. A cooker is the stove or pressure pot. 'My mother is a good cook', not 'a good cooker'."),
("VRB-052","wipe – wiped – wiped – wiping","'Yesterday morning' is finished time, so use the past tense 'wiped'.","WIPE rhymes with SWIPE: a quick sideways stroke with a cloth.","'Wipe your feet' is said at the door before entering a house.","Write a sentence about what you wipe after eating.",("see a spill","rub it with a cloth","the table is clean and dry"),"wipe vs wash","Wipe = clean by rubbing, usually with a cloth. Wash = clean with water."),
("VRB-053","shop – shopped – shopped – shopping","For a habit, use 'go shopping' or 'shop'. '-ing' alone can't be the main verb.","SHOP is both the place and the action: you shop at a shop.","'I'm just looking' is what you say when a shopkeeper offers help and you're only browsing.","Write a sentence about where you usually go shopping.",("make a list","walk around the market and choose things","pay and carry the bags home"),"shop vs buy","Shop = the whole activity of looking and buying. Buy = pay for one thing. You can shop without buying."),
("VRB-054","rest – rested – rested – resting","A regular habit uses the simple present, not the continuous form.","REST is what you do in a REST room: stop and recover.","'Get some rest' is a caring thing to say to someone who is tired or ill.","Write a sentence about how you rest after a long day.",("feel tired","sit or lie down quietly","feel fresh again"),"rest vs relax","Rest = stop activity to recover energy. Relax = let go of stress. You can relax while doing something, but resting means stopping."),
("VRB-055","relax – relaxed – relaxed – relaxing","'Relax' is complete by itself, so 'yourself' isn't needed.","RELAX = RE + LAX: let your body become loose ('lax') again.","'Relax, it's fine' calms someone down. 'Relaxed' describes a person; 'relaxing' describes a thing.","Write a sentence about what helps you relax.",("feel stressed","breathe slowly or listen to music","feel calm"),"relaxed vs relaxing","Relaxed = how a person feels ('I feel relaxed'). Relaxing = what causes it ('a relaxing holiday')."),
("VRB-056","exercise – exercised – exercised – exercising","As a verb, 'exercise' works alone. 'Do exercise' sounds less natural than 'exercise' or 'do exercises'.","EX-ER-CISE: three syllables, like three sets at the gym.","'Exercise' is also a noun: 'Walking is good exercise.'","Write a sentence about how often you exercise.",("put on sports shoes","move your body for 30 minutes","feel fit"),"exercise vs practise","Exercise = move your body for health. Practise = repeat a skill to get better at it."),
("VRB-057","jog – jogged – jogged – jogging","'Yesterday' needs the past tense, and 'jog' doubles the g: jogged.","JOG = Just Going slowly, running.","'Jog someone's memory' means help them remember.","Write a sentence about where you like to jog.",("warm up","run slowly and steadily","cool down and stretch"),"jog vs run","Jog = run slowly for exercise. Run = move fast on foot, for any reason."),
("VRB-058","stretch – stretched – stretched – stretching","A finished past action needs the past tense 'stretched'.","STRETCH sounds long, like a stretched rubber band.","'Stretch your legs' can also mean go for a short walk.","Write a sentence about when you stretch during the day.",("feel stiff","pull your arms and legs long","feel loose"),"stretch vs extend","Stretch = pull your body or a material longer. Extend = make something longer in time or size (extend a deadline)."),
("VRB-059","shave – shaved – shaved – shaving","A finished past action needs the past tense 'shaved'.","SHAVE rhymes with WAVE: move the razor in smooth waves.","'A close shave' means a narrow escape from danger.","Write a sentence about how often a man in your family shaves.",("put on shaving cream","move the razor over the skin","wash the face"),"shave vs cut","Shave = remove hair close to the skin with a razor. Cut = make hair shorter with scissors."),
("VRB-060","comb – combed – combed – combing","A daily habit needs the simple present. '-ing' needs am/is/are before it.","COMB has a silent B, like a tooth hiding in the comb.","'Comb through' means search carefully: 'Police combed through the files.'","Write a sentence about when you comb your hair.",("hair is messy","run the comb through it","hair is neat"),"comb vs brush","A comb has one row of teeth; a brush has many bristles. You can comb or brush hair, but only brush your teeth."),
("VRB-061","iron – ironed – ironed – ironing","The present continuous needs 'am/is/are' before the -ing verb.","IRON is pronounced 'EYE-urn' — the R is almost silent.","Indians often say 'press' ('Press my shirt'). In standard English, 'iron' is more common.","Write a sentence about who irons clothes in your home.",("the shirt is creased","move the hot iron over it","the shirt is smooth"),"iron vs press","Both mean make clothes flat. 'Iron' is standard. 'Press' is common in India and in 'dry cleaning and pressing'."),
("VRB-062","sweep – swept – swept – sweeping","'Already' with a finished action needs the present perfect: 'have swept'.","SWEEP → SWEPT, like SLEEP → SLEPT and KEEP → KEPT.","'Sweep something under the carpet' means hide a problem.","Write a sentence about how often you sweep your room.",("see dust on the floor","move the broom across it","collect the dust and throw it away"),"sweep vs mop","Sweep = clean with a dry broom. Mop = clean with a wet mop."),
("VRB-063","vacuum – vacuumed – vacuumed – vacuuming","A finished past action needs -ed: vacuumed.","VACUUM has double U: two 'u's for the long 'yoo' sound.","Spelling: one C, two Us — vacuum.","Write a sentence about which room you would vacuum first.",("plug in the machine","move it over the carpet","empty the dust bag"),"vacuum vs sweep","Vacuum = clean with an electric machine that sucks dirt. Sweep = clean with a broom."),
("VRB-064","water – watered – watered – watering","A finished past action needs -ed: watered.","WATER is both the noun and the verb: you water plants with water.","'My mouth is watering' means you smell or see tasty food.","Write a sentence about which plants you water at home.",("the soil is dry","pour water around the plant","the plant looks fresh"),"water vs wet","Water (verb) = give water to plants on purpose. Wet = make something damp, often by accident."),
("VRB-065","feed – fed – fed – feeding","'Feed' is irregular: the past is 'fed', not 'feeded'.","FEED → FED: drop one E, like BLEED → BLED.","'Fed up' means bored or annoyed: 'I'm fed up with this traffic.'","Write a sentence about an animal you have fed.",("the cat is hungry","put food in its bowl","the cat eats happily"),"feed vs eat","You feed someone else. You eat yourself. 'I feed the baby'; 'The baby eats.'"),
("VRB-066","lock – locked – locked – locking","After 'to', use the base verb: to lock.","LOCK and KEY: a lock needs a key to open.","'Lock yourself out' means you left the key inside and can't get in.","Write a sentence about what you lock before leaving home.",("close the door","turn the key","the door can't open"),"lock vs close","Close = shut. Lock = shut so it can't be opened without a key or code."),
("VRB-067","unlock – unlocked – unlocked – unlocking","A finished past action needs -ed: unlocked.","UN + LOCK = the opposite of lock.","Phones: 'Unlock your screen'. Games: 'unlock a new level'.","Write a sentence about how you unlock your phone.",("the door is locked","turn the key","the door opens"),"unlock vs open","Unlock = remove the lock. Open = move the door. You unlock first, then open."),
("VRB-068","charge – charged – charged – charging","For a length of time, use 'for' with the present perfect continuous: 'has been charging for two hours'.","CHARGE: like a charging bull, full of energy.","'Charge' also means ask for money: 'They charged me ₹200.'","Write a sentence about how long your phone takes to charge.",("the battery is low","plug in the charger","the battery is full"),"charge vs recharge","Charge = fill a battery. Recharge = fill it again. In India, 'recharge' also means top up your mobile balance."),
("VRB-069","unplug – unplugged – unplugged – unplugging","'Unplug' already means disconnect, so don't add 'off'.","UN + PLUG = pull the plug out.","'Unplug' also means take a break from phones and the internet: 'I unplug on Sundays.'","Write a sentence about which device you unplug at night.",("the device is plugged in","pull the plug out of the socket","no power flows"),"unplug vs switch off","Switch off = stop the power with the switch. Unplug = take the plug out of the socket completely.","I unplugged off the TV.","I unplugged the TV."),
("VRB-070","park – parked – parked – parking","In standard English, 'parking' is the activity; the place is a 'car park' (UK) or 'parking lot' (US).","PARK your car the way you'd sit in a park: stop and stay.","'No parking' signs mean you can't leave a vehicle there.","Write a sentence about where you park your bike or car.",("find an empty space","drive into it slowly","turn off the engine and lock it"),"park vs stop","Stop = come to a halt, even for a moment. Park = stop and leave the vehicle there.","I parked the car in the parking.","I parked the car in the car park."),
("VRB-071","commute – commuted – commuted – commuting","For a length of time, use 'for' with the present perfect: 'have commuted for three years'.","COMMUTE: to and from work, every day, like a loop.","A 'commuter' is a person who travels to work daily.","Write a sentence about how long your daily commute takes.",("leave home","travel by bus or train","reach the office"),"commute vs travel","Commute = the regular daily trip to work or college. Travel = any journey, often long or occasional."),
("VRB-072","depart – departed – departed – departing","Use 'depart for' + destination, or 'depart from' + starting point.","DE-PART: you become apart from the place you leave.","'Departures' is the airport sign for flights leaving.","Write a sentence about when your train or bus departs.",("wait on the platform","the train starts moving","the station is left behind"),"depart vs leave","Same meaning. Depart is formal (signs, tickets). Leave is everyday."),
("VRB-073","pack – packed – packed – packing","With 'since', use the present perfect continuous: 'have been packing'.","PACK your bag like a PACKet: everything tight inside.","'Pack up' means finish and put things away.","Write a sentence about what you always pack for a trip.",("collect clothes","fold them into the bag","close the bag"),"pack vs unpack","Pack = put things into a bag. Unpack = take them out."),
("VRB-074","unpack – unpacked – unpacked – unpacking","A finished past action needs -ed: unpacked.","UN + PACK = the opposite of pack.","'Let's unpack this idea' means examine it in detail.","Write a sentence about how soon you unpack after a trip.",("reach home","open the bag and take things out","put everything away"),"unpack vs empty","Unpack = take things out and put them away. Empty = make something have nothing inside."),
("VRB-075","tidy – tidied – tidied – tidying","After a consonant, final -y becomes -i before -ed: tidied.","TIDY and TIDIED: the Y hides as an I before -ed.","'Tidy up' is very common: 'Tidy up your room before guests come.'","Write a sentence about how often you tidy your desk.",("the room is messy","put things back in place","the room looks neat"),"tidy vs clean","Tidy = put things in order. Clean = remove dirt. A room can be tidy but dusty."),
("VRB-076","shower – showered – showered – showering","For a length of time, use 'for', not 'since', with the present perfect continuous.","SHOWER sounds like 'shout-er' without the T — rhymes with 'power'.","'Take a shower' (US) or 'have a shower' (UK) are more common than 'shower' alone.","Write a sentence about whether you prefer a morning or evening shower.",("turn on the water","wash under the running water","dry yourself with a towel"),"shower vs bath","Shower = standing under running water. Bath = sitting in a tub of water. In India, 'take a bath' often means any wash."),
("VRB-077","nap – napped – napped – napping","A habit uses the simple present, and 'usually' goes before the main verb.","NAP is a small word for a small sleep.","'Power nap' = a 15–20 minute nap to feel fresh.","Write a sentence about when you like to take a nap.",("feel sleepy after lunch","sleep for 20 minutes","wake up fresh"),"nap vs sleep","A nap is short, usually in the daytime. Sleep is the long rest at night."),
("VRB-078","snooze – snoozed – snoozed – snoozing","For a length of time, use 'for' with the present perfect continuous.","The SNOOZE button on the alarm gives you a few more minutes of sleep.","'You snooze, you lose' means if you're slow, you miss the chance.","Write a sentence about how many times you press snooze in the morning.",("the alarm rings","press snooze and close your eyes","the alarm rings again"),"snooze vs nap","A nap is planned. Snoozing is light, often accidental sleep, or delaying an alarm."),
("VRB-079","mop – mopped – mopped – mopping","Short words ending in one vowel + one consonant double the consonant: mop → mopped.","MOP → MOPPED, like STOP → STOPPED.","'Mop up' means clean up the last of something.","Write a sentence about which floor in your home gets mopped daily.",("the floor is dirty","wipe it with a wet mop","the floor dries clean"),"mop vs sweep","Sweep = dry broom. Mop = wet cloth on a stick. You usually sweep first, then mop."),
("VRB-080","dust – dusted – dusted – dusting","For something not done since a time, use the negative present perfect: 'haven't dusted since'.","DUST is both the dirt and the action of removing it.","'Dust off' means start using something again after a long time.","Write a sentence about what gets dusty fastest in your room.",("see dust on the shelf","wipe it with a dry cloth","the shelf shines"),"dust vs clean","Dust = remove dry dust with a cloth. Clean is general and can use water or soap."),
("VRB-081","walk – walked – walked – walking","A finished past action needs -ed: walked.","WALK has a silent L: say 'wawk'.","'Walk someone home' means go with them for safety or company.","Write a sentence about how far you walk every day.",("put on shoes","move on foot step by step","reach the place"),"walk vs go","Go = move to a place in any way. Walk = go on foot. 'I go to school' doesn't say how; 'I walk to school' does."),
("VRB-082","run – ran – run – running","'Run' is irregular: past 'ran', past participle 'run'.","RUN → RAN → RUN: the vowel changes and comes back, like a runner doing a lap.","'Run' also means manage: 'She runs a small shop.'","Write a sentence about a time you ran to catch a bus or train.",("see the bus leaving","move fast on foot","reach it just in time"),"run vs jog","Run = move fast on foot. Jog = run slowly, usually for exercise."),
("VRB-083","jump – jumped – jumped – jumping","'Over' is enough; don't add 'of' after it.","JUMP sounds like the thump when you land.","'Jump the queue' means go ahead of people who were waiting.","Write a sentence about the highest thing you have jumped over.",("bend your knees","push off the ground","land on your feet"),"jump vs hop","Jump = push off with both feet. Hop = jump on one foot, or make small quick jumps."),
("VRB-084","climb – climbed – climbed – climbing","'Climb' already means go up, so 'climb up on' is not needed; 'climb the mountain' is enough.","CLIMB has a silent B: say 'klime'.","'Climb down' can mean admit you were wrong.","Write a sentence about a hill, tree or wall you have climbed.",("stand at the bottom","use hands and feet to go up","reach the top"),"climb vs raise","You climb (go up) yourself. You raise (lift) something else."),
("VRB-085","crawl – crawled – crawled – crawling","With a length of time, use 'for' and the present perfect continuous: 'has been crawling for three months'.","CRAWL like a CRAB on the ground: low and slow.","'Traffic was crawling' means it was moving very slowly.","Write a sentence about something that crawls in your garden.",("the baby sits","moves on hands and knees","reaches the toy"),"crawl vs creep","Crawl = move on hands and knees. Creep = move slowly and quietly so no one notices."),
("VRB-086","swim – swam – swum – swimming","'Swim' is irregular: swim – swam – swum.","SWIM → SWAM → SWUM, like SING → SANG → SUNG.","'Go swimming' is more common than 'go for swim'.","Write a sentence about where you learned to swim, or would like to.",("get into the water","move arms and legs","reach the other side"),"swim vs float","Swim = move through water. Float = stay on top of the water without moving forward."),
("VRB-087","fly – flew – flown – flying","'Fly' is irregular: fly – flew – flown.","FLY → FLEW → FLOWN, like KNOW → KNEW → KNOWN.","'Time flies' means time passes quickly.","Write a sentence about a place you have flown to, or want to.",("board the plane","the plane goes up into the air","land in another city"),"fly vs flow","Fly = move through the air. Flow = move smoothly like water. Past tenses: flew / flowed."),
("VRB-088","drive – drove – driven – driving","'Drive' is irregular: drive – drove – driven.","DRIVE → DROVE → DRIVEN, like RIDE → RODE → RIDDEN.","'Drive someone crazy' means annoy them a lot.","Write a sentence about who drives in your family.",("start the car","steer on the road","park at the destination"),"drive vs ride","You drive a car (you control it from inside). You ride a bike, horse or bus."),
("VRB-089","ride – rode – ridden – riding","'Ride' is irregular: ride – rode – ridden.","RIDE → RODE → RIDDEN, like DRIVE → DROVE → DRIVEN.","'Give someone a ride' (US) or 'a lift' (UK) means take them in your vehicle.","Write a sentence about the first time you rode a bicycle.",("get on the bicycle","pedal along the road","get off at the park"),"ride vs drive","Ride = sit on or in something that moves (bike, horse, bus). Drive = control a car or truck."),
("VRB-090","travel – travelled/traveled – travelled/traveled – travelling/traveling","A finished past action needs the past tense. UK doubles the L (travelled); US doesn't (traveled).","TRAVEL has 'VEL' like velocity: going places.","'Travel light' means carry very little luggage.","Write a sentence about the longest journey you have travelled.",("book tickets","go by train or plane","reach a new place"),"travel vs journey","Travel is usually the verb (I travel). Journey is usually the noun (a long journey)."),
("VRB-091","wander – wandered – wandered – wandering","'Lose' is a verb (misplace). The adjective meaning 'unable to find your way' is 'lost'.","WANDER has an A, like 'walking Around'.","'My mind wandered' means you stopped paying attention.","Write a sentence about a place where you like to wander.",("walk into a market","move around with no plan","find something interesting"),"wander vs wonder","Wander (/ˈwɒndə/) = walk around without a plan. Wonder (/ˈwʌndə/) = think curiously."),
("VRB-092","chase – chased – chased – chasing","'Chase' takes a direct object. 'Chase after' is also fine, but never 'after of'.","CHASE rhymes with RACE: a chase is a race to catch someone.","'Chase your dreams' means try hard to achieve them.","Write a sentence about something a dog or child chased.",("see a cat","run after it","it climbs a tree"),"chase vs follow","Follow = go behind someone. Chase = run after them fast to catch them."),
("VRB-093","follow – followed – followed – following","A polite request doesn't take a question tag like 'isn't it'.","FOLLOW has two Ls, like two people walking one after the other.","Social media: 'Follow me on Instagram.' Also: 'Did you follow that?' = did you understand?","Write a sentence about a rule you always follow.",("the guide walks ahead","walk behind the guide","reach the room"),"follow vs chase","Follow = go behind at a normal pace. Chase = run after someone to catch them.","Please follow me to the room, isn't it?","Please follow me to the room."),
("VRB-094","lead – led – led – leading","'Lead' is irregular: the past is 'led', not 'leaded'.","LEAD (/liːd/) → LED (/led/), like FEED → FED.","A 'team lead' is the person in charge of a small team.","Write a sentence about a time you led a group or team.",("the team needs direction","walk in front and guide them","reach the goal together"),"lead vs led","Lead (/liːd/) is present. Led (/led/) is past. Lead (/led/) is also a metal, which is why people misspell the past tense."),
("VRB-095","push – pushed – pushed – pushing","'Push' takes a direct object, with no 'to'.","PUSH = press forward, away from you, like the door sign.","'Don't push me' means don't pressure me.","Write a sentence about something you pushed that was very heavy.",("put your hands on the door","press forward","the door opens"),"push vs pull","Push = move away from you. Pull = move towards you. Doors often say PUSH or PULL."),
("VRB-096","pull – pulled – pulled – pulling","'Pull' takes a direct object, with no 'to'.","PULL has two Ls, like two hands pulling a rope.","'Pull someone's leg' means joke with them.","Write a sentence about something you pull open every day.",("hold the handle","move it towards you","the drawer opens"),"pull vs drag","Pull = move towards you, often easily. Drag = pull something heavy along the ground with effort."),
("VRB-097","drag – dragged – dragged – dragging","A finished action needs the past tense, and 'drag' doubles the g: dragged.","DRAG sounds heavy and slow, like the scrape on the floor.","Computers: 'drag and drop' a file. 'The meeting dragged on' = it felt too long.","Write a sentence about something heavy you once dragged.",("the sofa is too heavy to lift","pull it slowly along the floor","it reaches the corner"),"drag vs carry","Carry = lift and hold while moving. Drag = pull along the ground without lifting."),
("VRB-098","lift – lifted – lifted – lifting","A finished past action needs -ed: lifted.","LIFT is also the Indian and British word for an elevator: it lifts you up.","'Give someone a lift' (UK) means give them a ride.","Write a sentence about the heaviest thing you can lift.",("bend your knees","raise the box with your arms","put it on the table"),"lift vs raise","Lift = pick something up physically. Raise = move up, often more formal (raise your hand, raise a price)."),
("VRB-099","drop – dropped – dropped – dropping","Simple past with a specific past time ('this morning') doesn't take 'since'.","DROP → DROPPED: double P before -ed.","'Drop someone at the station' means take them there in your vehicle. 'Drop by' = visit briefly.","Write a sentence about something you once dropped and broke.",("hold a glass","it slips from your hand","it breaks on the floor"),"drop vs fall","You drop something (you let it go). Something falls by itself. 'I dropped the cup; the cup fell.'"),
("VRB-100","throw – threw – thrown – throwing","'Throw' is irregular: throw – threw – thrown.","THROW → THREW → THROWN, like GROW → GREW → GROWN.","'Throw a party' means organise one. 'Throw away' = put in the bin.","Write a sentence about a game where you throw a ball.",("hold the ball","swing your arm forward","the ball flies away"),"throw vs through","Threw (past of throw) and through (from one side to the other) sound the same: 'He threw the ball through the window.'"),
]
